1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,581
Every Monday, I ask myself three questions.

2
00:00:03,967 --> 00:00:08,217
Who should I buy from? Can I afford it? Who still owes me?

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,886
Now I just ask BizOS, and it pays through PayPal.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:17,820
Each answer lived in a different place.

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,900
Supplier websites, my spreadsheets, and my PayPal account.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,790
It took my whole Monday. And I'm not the only one.

7
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:30,998
Small businesses are owed three hundred fifty-five billion dollars.

8
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,640
So this Monday, I tried something new.

9
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:38,524
My best seller is running low, and my supplier is late.

10
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,772
I press one shortcut, over any app, and ask.

11
00:00:42,267 --> 00:00:46,075
BizOS writes a plan first. Nothing runs until I've seen it.

12
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,038
Then it opens each supplier's website, and reads it like I would.

13
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:54,233
Before it confirms an order, it asks me.

14
00:00:54,633 --> 00:00:58,919
Then it builds my answer live, as a dashboard in AG Grid.

15
00:00:59,333 --> 00:01:03,573
The cheapest supplier is often late. Late means empty shelves.

16
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:08,754
Counting lost sales, the right supplier saves me thirteen thousand dollars.

17
00:01:09,167 --> 00:01:14,047
This isn't just a chart. The AI builds a whole AG Studio workspace.

18
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:18,913
It even adds its own widgets to AG Studio, like PayPal approvals.

19
00:01:19,333 --> 00:01:22,896
Click an idea, and the dashboard asks the next question.

20
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:26,624
Every question gets its own design. And my own edits stay.

21
00:01:27,667 --> 00:01:29,804
Good supplier. But can I afford it?

22
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:36,613
BizOS reads my real PayPal balance, even money already on its way out.

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,730
It keeps a safety cushion. Today, the supplier's deposit fits.

24
00:01:41,167 --> 00:01:43,497
The rest waits for the money people owe me.

25
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:47,890
One of my customers is weeks late.

26
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,500
BizOS sends them a PayPal invoice, with a pay button.

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,600
Then I hold to approve, and PayPal pays my supplier's deposit.

28
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,870
Refunds and disputes land in my dashboard too, ready to approve.

29
00:02:02,267 --> 00:02:07,271
BizOS reads PayPal by itself. Money only moves when I say so.

30
00:02:07,667 --> 00:02:12,387
Due dates go on my calendar. Money in, money out, all through PayPal.

31
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:15,876
Next Monday, my question is different.

32
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:21,373
It searches real supplier websites. No price? It asks for a quote.

33
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:26,327
Even my finance question works: do my PayPal payments match my invoices?

34
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:28,527
So what changes?

35
00:02:28,933 --> 00:02:33,173
A late invoice with a PayPal pay button can get paid two weeks sooner.

36
00:02:33,567 --> 00:02:37,624
Not one payment over budget. And hours of chasing, gone.

37
00:02:38,033 --> 00:02:42,673
Across millions of small businesses, that's billions of dollars, sooner.

38
00:02:43,367 --> 00:02:45,253
My whole Monday, on one page.

39
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,300
Monday used to take all day. Now it takes one question.

40
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:55,742
BizOS. Ask. Check. Approve.
